/* Editorial rebuild: first cultural-myth dossiers.  These replace the compact launch template. */
(function () {
  const profiles = {
    'p-diddy': {
      description: 'Sean Combs built Bad Boy Entertainment into one of the defining New York music companies of the 1990s. His extreme visibility—as producer, performer, executive, fashion businessman, and television personality—also made him a frequent subject of online narratives about celebrity power and secret influence.',
      biography: 'Born Sean John Combs in Harlem in 1969, he grew up in Mount Vernon, New York, and attended Howard University before leaving to work at Uptown Records. There he assisted Andre Harrell and helped develop acts including Jodeci and Mary J. Blige. In 1993 he founded Bad Boy Records, initially from a small Manhattan office, and made a durable partnership with Christopher Wallace, the Notorious B.I.G.\n\nBad Boy’s success rested on a sharply styled mix of hip-hop, R&B, sampling, fashion, videos, and relentless promotion. Combs later recorded under Puff Daddy, P. Diddy, and Diddy; expanded into Sean John clothing, Cîroc promotion, television, and other ventures; and became one of the era’s best-known examples of the artist-executive. The scale of that public persona is central to the mythology collected around him.',
      history: 'His career unfolded during the consolidation of rap as a major commercial industry, when labels, radio, video channels, nightlife, and fashion became tightly linked. The East Coast–West Coast conflict of the mid-1990s formed a tragic part of Bad Boy’s period of ascent after the deaths of Tupac Shakur and Wallace. Later publicity, lawsuits, and criminal proceedings drew renewed attention to Combs’s network and legacy.',
      works: 'Bad Boy Records issued landmark releases by the Notorious B.I.G., Faith Evans, 112, Total, Mase, and others. Combs’s 1997 album <em>No Way Out</em> won the Grammy Award for Best Rap Album; its single “I’ll Be Missing You” became a global hit. <em>Making the Band</em> turned record-company selection into reality television, while Sean John made his name visible outside music.',
      ideas: 'Combs has not published an occult teaching or claimed a ritual lineage. The recurring themes in his own public work are transformation, success, loyalty, luxury, and self-invention. Internet readings often treat the visual language of high-budget music culture—formal dress, black-and-white photography, elite parties, numerology, and logo design—as coded symbolism; in this dossier those readings belong to the cultural afterlife of celebrity, not to a system Combs set out in writing.',
      influence: 'He helped establish the modern image of the hip-hop entrepreneur: an artist or producer who moves among recording, publishing, fashion, liquor advertising, television, and personal branding. Bad Boy’s sound and image influenced late-1990s rap and R&B, while his business model became a reference point for later executives and performers.',
      controversies: 'Combs’s career has included highly public legal disputes and, in 2024, federal charges that he has denied. Those developments generated a much larger wave of social-media material that often joins real reporting to unverified claims about hidden rites, trafficking networks, or symbolic “proof.” The useful distinction is between public documents and the stories built around them: the latter reveal anxieties about fame, access, and power, but are not a biography of an occult order.',
      legacy: 'Whether approached through music history, fashion, or the folklore of the celebrity internet, Combs is a major figure in the architecture of modern fame. His entry belongs in the cultural-myth section because it traces how a public image can become a screen onto which strangers project systems of secrecy.',
      sources: '<ul><li>Recording Academy, “Sean Combs” artist materials</li><li>Jeff Chang, <em>Can’t Stop Won’t Stop</em></li><li>Ben Westhoff, <em>Original Gangstas</em></li><li>Bad Boy Records release archives</li><li>Billboard, Bad Boy and Combs career reporting</li><li>United States Department of Justice, public filings concerning Sean Combs</li></ul>'
    },
    'jeffrey-epstein': {
      description: 'Jeffrey Epstein was a financier whose social access to wealthy and famous people, criminal prosecution, and death in federal custody produced one of the most extensive conspiracy mythologies of the internet age.',
      biography: 'Jeffrey Edward Epstein was born in Brooklyn in 1953 and began his career teaching mathematics and physics at the Dalton School in Manhattan. He then worked at Bear Stearns before establishing private financial businesses. By the 1990s he cultivated an image of unusual wealth and access, moving among Palm Beach, Manhattan, New Mexico, Paris, and the U.S. Virgin Islands.\n\nThe documented core of his life is inseparable from abuse. In 2008 he pleaded guilty in Florida to procuring a minor for prostitution and solicitation of prostitution. In July 2019 federal prosecutors in New York charged him with sex trafficking of minors and conspiracy. He died in custody before trial, and the criminal case against him ended with his death.',
      history: 'Epstein’s story developed during an era when private wealth, exclusive philanthropy, elite universities, and celebrity media could create powerful social legitimacy with little public scrutiny. His 2007–08 non-prosecution agreement became a major public controversy; the 2019 prosecution reopened attention to survivors’ accounts and to the institutions and individuals who had associated with him.',
      works: 'There is no major intellectual or artistic corpus to interpret. His public record consists chiefly of business entities, philanthropy announcements, property holdings, litigation, and criminal case materials. The absence of a substantive public body of work is itself part of the mystery people attached to him: an influential social presence with a deliberately opaque financial biography.',
      ideas: 'Epstein did not establish a recognized occult school. The “occult” element of his legend grew from the architecture and isolation of some of his properties, the language of secret societies used online, and the tendency of conspiracy culture to treat elite social networks as evidence of a hidden religion. Those motifs are better understood as modern political folklore—ritualized images of forbidden access—than as a known belief system.',
      influence: 'The case changed conversations about survivor advocacy, prosecutorial discretion, institutional due diligence, and the way wealth can protect a public reputation. It also became a template for online investigations that mix court records, flight logs, photographs, rumor, and speculation into a single expanding narrative.',
      controversies: 'The verified controversies concern abuse, the 2008 plea agreement, and the 2019 federal prosecution. His death generated immediate suspicion because of jail failures documented by federal investigators, but speculation often outruns the available record. The cultural-myth dossier follows how “Epstein” became shorthand for elite impunity and hidden networks without turning every person photographed with him into a participant in his crimes.',
      legacy: 'Epstein remains a cautionary symbol of prestige without accountability. The persistence of the conspiracy material around him shows why a dossier needs both documentary discipline and an understanding of myth: the story is not only what occurred, but also how images of secrecy are now recycled across unrelated claims.',
      sources: '<ul><li>U.S. Attorney’s Office, Southern District of New York, 2019 indictment announcement</li><li>U.S. Department of Justice Office of the Inspector General, Metropolitan Correctional Center report</li><li>Miami Herald, Julie K. Brown’s Epstein investigation</li><li>United States v. Jeffrey Epstein, federal court filings</li><li>New York State sex-offender registry materials</li><li>Vicky Ward, <em>The Lifestyles of Jeffrey Epstein</em></li></ul>'
    },
    'ghislaine-maxwell': {
      description: 'Ghislaine Maxwell, daughter of publisher Robert Maxwell, moved through elite social circles in Britain and the United States before her conviction for crimes connected with Jeffrey Epstein. Her name became embedded in a much wider mythology about influence and hidden networks.',
      biography: 'Ghislaine Noelle Marion Maxwell was born in France in 1961 and grew up in Britain, where her father Robert Maxwell built a publishing empire. She studied at Balliol College, Oxford, worked in the Maxwell media businesses, and became a familiar figure in London society. After her father’s 1991 death and the revelation of severe financial irregularities at his companies, she relocated to New York.\n\nIn the United States she became closely associated with Epstein. In December 2021 a federal jury convicted Maxwell of sex trafficking and other offenses arising from her role in recruiting and grooming underage girls for Epstein. She was sentenced in 2022 to twenty years in prison.',
      history: 'Her life joins two histories of late twentieth-century elite scandal: the collapse of the Maxwell publishing empire and the Epstein criminal case. Her cultivated social standing—charities, parties, art, universities, and prominent acquaintances—later became raw material for broad conspiracy maps that often flatten many different relationships into a single secret structure.',
      works: 'Maxwell’s early public work included positions in the Maxwell family’s publishing and football ventures. In New York she participated in philanthropic and environmental projects, including the TerraMar Project. These visible initiatives are part of the public record, though they were later re-examined in light of her relationship with Epstein and her conviction.',
      ideas: 'She has no recognized occult writings or organization. Online narratives attach symbolic importance to photographs, event lists, elite clubs, and the surname Maxwell. Such readings tend to use “secret society” as a language for exclusion and power. This entry records that cultural mechanism while keeping it distinct from the evidence presented at trial.',
      influence: 'The prosecution made Maxwell a central figure in public discussion of facilitation, recruitment, and the social labor that can surround abuse. It also renewed scrutiny of the confidence people place in prestige institutions and private social networks.',
      controversies: 'The central controversy is not symbolic but legal: survivors’ testimony and the federal prosecution that ended in her conviction. Separate internet claims connect her to sweeping occult or political plots; they usually grow by association rather than by a traceable chain of evidence. The more revealing question is why the image of the well-connected hostess became so powerful in the conspiracy imagination.',
      legacy: 'Maxwell’s name has become a byword for the meeting point of money, social access, and concealment. In the cultural-myth archive, her dossier helps explain how a documented criminal case accumulates an ever-larger speculative shadow.',
      sources: '<ul><li>United States v. Ghislaine Maxwell, trial record and verdict</li><li>U.S. Attorney’s Office, Southern District of New York, case releases</li><li>U.S. Department of Justice, sentencing announcement</li><li>Tom Bower, <em>Maxwell: The Final Verdict</em></li><li>New York Times, Maxwell trial coverage</li><li>BBC News, Robert Maxwell and Ghislaine Maxwell background reporting</li></ul>'
    },
    'john-f-kennedy': {
      description: 'John Fitzgerald Kennedy, the thirty-fifth president of the United States, became an enduring focus of political suspicion after his assassination in Dallas on 22 November 1963. The open wound of that event made him a central figure in American conspiracy culture.',
      biography: 'Born in Brookline, Massachusetts, in 1917, Kennedy served in the U.S. Navy during the Second World War and became known for the survival of PT-109 in the Solomon Islands. He entered Congress in 1947, served as senator from Massachusetts, and won the presidency in 1960. His administration faced the Bay of Pigs invasion, the Cuban Missile Crisis, civil-rights conflict, and the accelerating Cold War.\n\nKennedy was killed while riding in a motorcade in Dallas. Lee Harvey Oswald was arrested and, two days later, murdered by Jack Ruby. The speed and violence of those events, combined with incomplete public confidence in official investigations, ensured that the assassination would never remain only a historical event.',
      history: 'Kennedy governed at the height of nuclear anxiety. His public language of a “New Frontier,” the Peace Corps, the space program, and civil rights gave the presidency a symbolic force that survived his death. The Warren Commission concluded that Oswald acted alone; the House Select Committee on Assassinations later found that Kennedy was probably assassinated as a result of a conspiracy, while unable to identify the other gunman or participants.',
      works: 'Kennedy’s public record includes <em>Why England Slept</em>, <em>Profiles in Courage</em>, inaugural and Berlin speeches, the American University peace address, and the 1962 Rice University speech committing the United States to reach the Moon. These texts made his political career unusually literary and symbolic, feeding his later image as a fallen king of modern America.',
      ideas: 'Kennedy did not teach occult doctrines. The “occult” quality of his afterlife lies in a modern civic mythology of sacrifice, hidden enemies, coded warnings, and an interrupted national destiny. Researchers and storytellers have read Cold War secrecy, intelligence agencies, Cuba, organized crime, and the military-industrial complex into the assassination; these are competing political narratives, not a uniform esoteric tradition.',
      influence: 'The Kennedy presidency shaped diplomacy during the Cuban Missile Crisis, national investment in space exploration, and the political language of generational renewal. His assassination transformed journalism, historical research, film, and public trust in government.',
      controversies: 'Questions about the Warren Report, classified records, and intelligence operations have kept the case alive for six decades. Some theories are carefully argued from documents; others build immense systems from visual coincidence. The dossier preserves the distinction while recognizing that the assassination’s mystery is itself a major cultural force.',
      legacy: 'Kennedy remains both a historical president and a ritual figure in American memory. His life is repeatedly retold as promise cut short, and Dallas remains a site where factual investigation and national myth meet.',
      sources: '<ul><li>John F. Kennedy Presidential Library and Museum, biography and speeches</li><li>Warren Commission, <em>Report of the President’s Commission on the Assassination of President Kennedy</em></li><li>House Select Committee on Assassinations, final report</li><li>National Archives, JFK Assassination Records Collection</li><li>Robert Dallek, <em>An Unfinished Life</em></li><li>Fredrik Logevall, <em>JFK: Coming of Age in the American Century</em></li></ul>'
    },
    'marilyn-monroe': {
      description: 'Marilyn Monroe transformed Norma Jeane Mortenson’s difficult early life into one of the most recognizable screen identities in the world. Her death in 1962 made her a lasting object of political, romantic, and occult-adjacent cultural myth.',
      biography: 'Born in Los Angeles in 1926, Norma Jeane spent much of her childhood in foster homes and an orphanage. A wartime factory photograph led to modeling, and in 1946 she signed with Twentieth Century-Fox, adopting the name Marilyn Monroe. She studied acting, worked persistently through minor parts, and became a major star with <em>Gentlemen Prefer Blondes</em>, <em>How to Marry a Millionaire</em>, <em>The Seven Year Itch</em>, and <em>Some Like It Hot</em>.\n\nMonroe also pursued more serious work, forming Marilyn Monroe Productions and studying at the Actors Studio. Her marriages to Joe DiMaggio and Arthur Miller, friendships with writers and artists, public struggles with illness and dependency, and death on 5 August 1962 produced a life story that was immediately turned into legend.',
      history: 'Hollywood’s studio system made and constrained Monroe. She learned to use the blonde persona as a performance while fighting for better roles and contractual control. Her death occurred during the Kennedy era, allowing her name to become permanently linked—often through conjecture—to powerful men and political secrecy.',
      works: 'Her screen performances range from the comic precision of Lorelei Lee in <em>Gentlemen Prefer Blondes</em> to the vulnerable Sugar Kane in <em>Some Like It Hot</em>. She recorded “Diamonds Are a Girl’s Best Friend,” appeared at President Kennedy’s 1962 birthday gala, and left unfinished work on <em>Something’s Got to Give</em>. Her photographs with Richard Avedon, Cecil Beaton, and Milton Greene became part of the Monroe archive as much as the films.',
      ideas: 'Monroe did not present herself as an occult practitioner. Yet her image is often handled like an icon: endlessly reproduced, invested with erotic power, grief, glamour, and resurrection. Conspiracy narratives use her as a tragic witness to secret politics; occult-themed art uses the same image as a modern goddess, martyr, or Hollywood ghost.',
      influence: 'She changed screen performance, fashion, beauty culture, and the language of celebrity. Later performers from Madonna to countless photographers have borrowed the Monroe image, often to question the distinction between person and manufactured symbol.',
      controversies: 'Her death was ruled a probable suicide by the Los Angeles County coroner. The links drawn to John and Robert Kennedy have encouraged many rival stories, including murder and cover-up narratives. These claims have become part of the Monroe industry, but the enduring material record is her work, her journals, and the accounts of an overworked performer trying to control her own image.',
      legacy: 'Monroe’s legacy rests on a rare combination of comedy, vulnerability, intelligence, and photographic immortality. In conspiracy culture she is a perpetual unsolved clue; in film history she remains a rigorous comic actor and a figure who exposed the cost of being made into a symbol.',
      sources: '<ul><li>Marilyn Monroe Collection, Library of Congress</li><li>Donald Spoto, <em>Marilyn Monroe: The Biography</em></li><li>Lois Banner, <em>Marilyn: The Passion and the Paradox</em></li><li>Sarah Churchwell, <em>The Many Lives of Marilyn Monroe</em></li><li>Academy Museum of Motion Pictures, Monroe materials</li><li>Los Angeles County Coroner’s report, 1962</li></ul>'
    },
    'elvis-presley': { description:'Elvis Presley fused regional American music into an unprecedented mass phenomenon. His early death and the intensity of his fan culture produced stories of survival, spiritual searching, and a “King” who never quite left the stage.', biography:'Elvis Aaron Presley was born in Tupelo, Mississippi, in 1935 and moved with his family to Memphis in 1948. At Sun Records, Sam Phillips, Scotty Moore, and Bill Black helped him turn blues, country, gospel, and rhythm-and-blues material into an electrifying new performance style. RCA purchased his contract in 1955; by the following year “Heartbreak Hotel,” television appearances, and films had made him a national sensation.\n\nMilitary service, Hollywood contracts, the 1968 Comeback Special, and his Las Vegas residencies marked distinct reinventions. Presley died at Graceland in 1977, aged forty-two, after years of health problems and prescription-drug use. The suddenness of his death gave his public afterlife an almost religious intensity.', history:'Presley emerged when American radio and youth culture were crossing racial and regional boundaries, often uneasily. His rise provoked moral panic as well as devotion. The postwar entertainment system then transformed him into a global commodity; Graceland became a shrine long before the language of pop pilgrimage was common.', works:'Key recordings include “That’s All Right,” “Heartbreak Hotel,” “Hound Dog,” “Jailhouse Rock,” “Suspicious Minds,” and the gospel album <em>How Great Thou Art</em>. His films were uneven but made him internationally visible. The 1968 television special and the live album <em>From Elvis in Memphis</em> demonstrated his ability to return to rawer musical forms.', ideas:'Presley read widely in popular religion, comparative spirituality, and self-help, and kept books on metaphysics at Graceland. That documented curiosity has been exaggerated into elaborate claims of initiation and hidden knowledge. The more compelling story is his lifelong attraction to gospel feeling, mystical language, and the search for personal transformation amid fame.', influence:'He altered vocal phrasing, stage movement, recording economics, fashion, and the meaning of a teenage audience. Musicians across rock, country, soul, and punk treated his early recordings as foundational.', controversies:'Arguments about cultural borrowing, racial credit, drugs, and management by Colonel Tom Parker are central to his history. After 1977, impersonators and alleged sightings made “Elvis lives” a durable American legend, a secular resurrection tale built on grief and image culture.', legacy:'Presley remains a genuine musical innovator and a modern folk saint. The conspiracy stories around his survival persist because his persona was so total that an ordinary death seemed insufficient to close it.', sources:'<ul><li>Graceland Archives, Presley biography and collections</li><li>Peter Guralnick, <em>Last Train to Memphis</em></li><li>Peter Guralnick, <em>Careless Love</em></li><li>Ernst Jorgensen, <em>Elvis Presley: A Life in Music</em></li><li>Sun Records historical archives</li><li>Rock & Roll Hall of Fame, Elvis Presley essay</li></ul>' },
    'michael-jackson': { description:'Michael Jackson was a singer, dancer, songwriter, and visual innovator whose fame became planetary. His intensely designed imagery invited symbolic readings that have continued long after his death.', biography:'Michael Joseph Jackson was born in Gary, Indiana, in 1958 and performed with the Jackson 5 while still a child. Motown made the group a household name; as a solo artist he worked with Quincy Jones to make <em>Off the Wall</em>, <em>Thriller</em>, and <em>Bad</em>. His voice, choreography, short films, and technical perfectionism remade the scale on which pop music could operate.\n\nJackson’s adult life was increasingly secluded and highly managed. He built Neverland Ranch, mounted enormous global tours, endured repeated public scrutiny, and died in Los Angeles in 2009 after an overdose of propofol administered by his physician.', history:'His career spanned the transition from radio and vinyl to MTV, cable, global touring, and celebrity surveillance. <em>Thriller</em> helped break barriers on MTV and showed that a music video could function as a mini-film with its own mythology.', works:'<em>Off the Wall</em>, <em>Thriller</em>, <em>Bad</em>, <em>Dangerous</em>, and <em>HIStory</em> are the core albums. Videos for “Billie Jean,” “Beat It,” “Thriller,” “Smooth Criminal,” and “Black or White” joined elaborate visual worlds to unforgettable music; the moonwalk at the 1983 Motown anniversary became a global image.', ideas:'Jackson spoke often about imagination, childhood, healing, spectacle, and racial unity. His videos use masks, doubles, transformation, the undead, and dream spaces—materials that invite occult or esoteric interpretation without constituting a declared occult practice. Fans and conspiracy channels have treated costume details and stage imagery as clues to hidden affiliations; the imagery is more securely understood as theatrical pop language shaped by film, dance, horror, and fashion.', influence:'Few artists have influenced music video, dance, stadium touring, fan culture, and global pop more deeply. His methods became standard practice for artists whose records depend on a coordinated visual universe.', controversies:'Allegations of child sexual abuse, the 2005 criminal trial in which he was acquitted, civil litigation, and documentaries have left a complex and painful legacy. His death generated a separate set of conspiratorial claims. A serious dossier must hold the public record and the cultural myth apart rather than use symbolism to settle either.', legacy:'Jackson remains an artist of immense technical and emotional impact, and a difficult public figure whose image cannot be separated from the arguments around it. The occult readings survive because his work made transformation visible: he repeatedly appeared as child, monster, angel, criminal, and ghost.', sources:'<ul><li>Michael Jackson Estate, official release archive</li><li>Nelson George, <em>Michael Jackson: The Ultimate Collection</em></li><li>J. Randy Taraborrelli, <em>Michael Jackson: The Magic and the Madness</em></li><li>Rock & Roll Hall of Fame, Michael Jackson materials</li><li>Los Angeles County Coroner, Michael Jackson death investigation</li><li>United States v. Michael Jackson, 2005 trial record</li></ul>' },
    'tupac-shakur': { description:'Tupac Shakur combined rap, poetry, acting, and political inheritance in a brief career that remains surrounded by rival accounts of violence, survival, and posthumous revelation.', biography:'Tupac Amaru Shakur was born in New York City in 1971 to parents connected with the Black Panther Party and later moved among Baltimore, Marin City, and Oakland. He studied acting, poetry, and dance at the Baltimore School for the Arts before joining Digital Underground and releasing <em>2Pacalypse Now</em> in 1991. His work was unusually direct about poverty, police violence, family, masculinity, and grief.\n\nBy the mid-1990s Shakur was both a major rapper and a film actor. He was shot in Las Vegas on 7 September 1996 and died six days later. The unresolved nature of the killing for many years, followed by later prosecution connected to the case, allowed a huge afterlife of claims that he escaped death or continued to communicate through lyrics.', history:'Shakur worked during the volatile growth of gangsta rap and the East Coast–West Coast conflict. His legal troubles, imprisonment, signing to Death Row Records, and conflict with other artists made him a central figure in a commercial and media battle that often obscured the range of his writing.', works:'<em>Me Against the World</em> and <em>All Eyez on Me</em> made him a defining artist of his generation. “Dear Mama,” “Keep Ya Head Up,” “Changes,” “California Love,” and “Hail Mary” show his range from intimate tribute to public prophecy. Films such as <em>Juice</em> and <em>Poetic Justice</em> revealed a strong screen presence.', ideas:'His name, political family history, references to resurrection, and lyrics about fate and surveillance have been read by listeners as evidence of hidden foreknowledge. More concretely, Shakur used spiritual and apocalyptic vocabulary to confront a world in which young Black lives felt disposable. The “Tupac lives” story is a folk response to a death that his audience found impossible to accept.', influence:'His writing influenced rappers, poets, activists, filmmakers, and scholars. He expanded what commercial rap could say about maternal love, incarceration, trauma, and political anger.', controversies:'His career included convictions, civil suits, feuds, and a highly contested public persona. The many conspiracy accounts of his death often attach symbolic certainty to ambiguous events. They are part of his cultural legacy, but they cannot replace the recordings and the documented conditions that shaped them.', legacy:'Shakur remains a living presence in music because his voice sounds unfinished rather than historical. His archive—songs, notebooks, interviews, and performances—continues to create the feeling that another message might still emerge.', sources:'<ul><li>Tupac Shakur Estate, official archive</li><li>Michael Eric Dyson, <em>Holler If You Hear Me</em></li><li>Kevin Powell, <em>Tupac: Resurrection</em></li><li>Stacey Robinson, <em>Tupac Shakur: The Life and Times of an American Icon</em></li><li>Los Angeles County District Attorney, 2023 Duane Davis prosecution materials</li><li>Library of Congress, Tupac Shakur collections</li></ul>' },
    'christopher-marlowe': { description:'Christopher Marlowe was a dramatist, translator, and poet whose violent death at twenty-nine turned a major Elizabethan writer into a permanent mystery figure.', biography:'Marlowe was baptized in Canterbury in 1564, the same year as Shakespeare, and studied at the King’s School and Corpus Christi College, Cambridge. His unusually delayed master’s degree later gave rise to reports that he had performed government service abroad. By the late 1580s he was writing plays of extraordinary verbal force for the London stage.\n\nHis career ended abruptly in 1593 when he was killed in Deptford during a gathering with Ingram Frizer, Nicholas Skeres, and Robert Poley. The coroner’s account described a quarrel over a bill, but the presence of men connected to espionage and the tense political atmosphere made the death an inexhaustible source of conjecture.', history:'Marlowe wrote in a Protestant, expansionist England alive with religious conflict, censorship, intelligence work, and theatre. The same London world produced daring drama and severe penalties for suspected atheism or sedition. A document known as the “Baines Note” accused him of blasphemous opinions, though its status and motives remain disputed.', works:'His major plays include <em>Tamburlaine</em>, <em>The Jew of Malta</em>, <em>Edward II</em>, and <em>The Tragical History of Doctor Faustus</em>. He translated Ovid and Lucan and wrote “The Passionate Shepherd to His Love.” <em>Doctor Faustus</em>, with its pact, spirits, and hunger for forbidden knowledge, is the central source of his occult reputation.', ideas:'Marlowe’s stage figures seek power, knowledge, and transformation at catastrophic scale. Faustus’s conjuring is dramatic theology rather than evidence that Marlowe practiced magic. Later writers nevertheless turned the author into a clandestine skeptic, spy, heretic, and magician—a persona made possible by his sharp language and unexplained death.', influence:'His blank verse and heroic protagonists prepared the way for Shakespeare and later English tragedy. The Faust legend became one of the great laboratories of European occult imagination.', controversies:'Questions about government service, atheism, homosexuality, the Baines accusations, and the Deptford killing have encouraged theories that he was murdered or survived under another identity. The claim that he secretly wrote Shakespeare’s plays is a modern authorship myth, not the conclusion of the documentary record.', legacy:'Marlowe endures as both a canonical playwright and an occult-literary emblem: the young author whose most famous character reaches for forbidden knowledge and pays for it with his soul.', sources:'<ul><li>Oxford Dictionary of National Biography, “Christopher Marlowe”</li><li>Charles Nicholl, <em>The Reckoning</em></li><li>J. A. Downie and J. T. Parnell, <em>Constructing Christopher Marlowe</em></li><li>British Library, Marlowe collection essays</li><li>National Archives, Deptford inquest material</li><li>Christopher Marlowe, <em>Doctor Faustus</em></li></ul>' },
    'william-shakespeare': { description:'William Shakespeare’s plays turned ghosts, prophecy, witchcraft, storms, and transformation into durable theatrical language. The gaps in his biography later made him a magnet for authorship and esoteric speculation.', biography:'Shakespeare was baptized at Stratford-upon-Avon in April 1564, married Anne Hathaway in 1582, and was established in London theatre by the early 1590s. He became actor, shareholder, and principal playwright for the Lord Chamberlain’s Men and later the King’s Men. His company performed at the Globe and the court; he returned to Stratford with substantial property and died there in 1616.\n\nThe surviving record is uneven but substantial: parish registers, legal documents, title pages, the First Folio, and testimony from fellow actors. The ordinary materiality of that record has not prevented later generations from imagining hidden identities behind the name.', history:'His career coincided with the Elizabethan and Jacobean stage, a period shaped by religious settlement, overseas expansion, plague closures, court patronage, and anxieties about treason. The 1606 Witchcraft Act and King James’s interest in demonology formed the background to <em>Macbeth</em> and other supernatural drama.', works:'The plays include <em>Hamlet</em>, <em>Othello</em>, <em>King Lear</em>, <em>Macbeth</em>, <em>A Midsummer Night’s Dream</em>, and <em>The Tempest</em>, as well as 154 sonnets and narrative poems. Prospero’s books, the Weird Sisters, Hamlet’s ghost, and the fairies of the comedies are theatrical devices that became a shared language for later occult writers.', ideas:'Shakespeare had no known occult manifesto. His work borrows from classical myth, chronicles, the Bible, popular magic, astrology, and Renaissance ideas of order and disorder. Readers have treated <em>The Tempest</em> as a Rosicrucian allegory and Shakespeare as a coded philosopher; such interpretations show the plays’ symbolic richness rather than proving membership in a secret fraternity.', influence:'No English-language writer has had a wider influence on theatre, language, education, and adaptation. Occult theatre, ritual drama, and modern fantasy continually return to his ghosts, witches, fairies, and magicians.', controversies:'The “authorship question” proposes Francis Bacon, Edward de Vere, Marlowe, or others as the real author. These claims thrive on the distance between Shakespeare’s documented life and the scope of the plays, but the contemporary publishing and theatrical evidence supports Shakespeare of Stratford.', legacy:'Shakespeare is a central ancestor of the Western magical imagination, even when he is not an occult author. His plays make supernatural encounter feel psychologically real, which is why every generation can recruit them for its own mysteries.', sources:'<ul><li>Folger Shakespeare Library, biography and First Folio resources</li><li>British Library, Shakespeare collection</li><li>Stanley Wells, <em>Shakespeare: A Life in Drama</em></li><li>James Shapiro, <em>Contested Will</em></li><li>William Shakespeare, <em>Macbeth</em> and <em>The Tempest</em></li><li>Oxford Dictionary of National Biography, “William Shakespeare”</li></ul>' },
    'francis-bacon': { description:'Francis Bacon was a lawyer, politician, essayist, and theorist of experimental knowledge. His program for a reformed science later drew Rosicrucian, Masonic, and authorship legends around his name.', biography:'Francis Bacon was born in London in 1561, educated at Trinity College, Cambridge, and Gray’s Inn, and entered Parliament at twenty-three. He rose under James I to attorney general, lord keeper, and lord chancellor, receiving the title Viscount St Alban. In 1621 he was convicted of taking bribes and removed from office, though he later received a pardon.\n\nAfter political disgrace, Bacon devoted himself to writing. He died in 1626 after an experiment involving the preservation of meat in snow, an ending perfectly suited to the public image of a man who made experiment into a moral vocation.', history:'Bacon wrote at the threshold of the Scientific Revolution, when natural philosophy, alchemy, navigation, state power, and printing were transforming European knowledge. He criticized inherited systems of learning and argued for cooperative observation, experiment, and useful invention.', works:'<em>The Advancement of Learning</em>, <em>Novum Organum</em>, <em>New Atlantis</em>, and the <em>Essays</em> form the core of his legacy. <em>New Atlantis</em> imagines Salomon’s House, an institution devoted to organized inquiry, and later readers repeatedly treated it as a blueprint for secret scientific brotherhoods.', ideas:'Bacon’s “idols” of the mind describe habits that distort judgment; his method sought knowledge through disciplined encounter with nature. He is often claimed as a Rosicrucian or cryptographic master because <em>New Atlantis</em> and the early Rosicrucian manifestos share a utopian atmosphere. No decisive documentary link establishes membership, but the association became fertile in esoteric reading.', influence:'The Royal Society and later advocates of empirical science frequently invoked Bacon as a founding spirit. His emphasis on institutions and collective inquiry helped shape the modern ideal of research.', controversies:'His political conduct, alleged ciphers, supposed authorship of Shakespeare’s works, and Rosicrucian affiliations generate competing Bacon mythologies. The cipher claims often depend on flexible methods that find messages wherever they look; nevertheless they tell an important story about the desire to locate a hidden architect behind English culture.', legacy:'Bacon remains both a real architect of modern intellectual method and a symbolic master of concealed knowledge. His imagined laboratory of Salomon’s House continues to look like a sacred temple of science.', sources:'<ul><li>Oxford Dictionary of National Biography, “Francis Bacon”</li><li>Francis Bacon, <em>Novum Organum</em></li><li>Francis Bacon, <em>New Atlantis</em></li><li>Brian Vickers, <em>Francis Bacon and Renaissance Prose</em></li><li>Lisa Jardine and Alan Stewart, <em>Hostage to Fortune</em></li><li>Royal Society, Bacon historical materials</li></ul>' },
    'leonardo-da-vinci': { description:'Leonardo da Vinci was a painter, engineer, anatomist, and natural philosopher whose notebooks made him an emblem of hidden knowledge. His curiosity later invited elaborate secret-society narratives.', biography:'Leonardo was born near Vinci in Tuscany in 1452 and trained in Florence in the workshop of Andrea del Verrocchio. He worked for Ludovico Sforza in Milan, later for Cesare Borgia and the French court, and died at Amboise in 1519. His surviving notebooks show a mind moving continuously among painting, mechanics, water, anatomy, flight, architecture, and optics.\n\nMany projects remained incomplete because Leonardo treated observation as a lifelong practice rather than a path to a single finished system. That incompletion, coupled with the dispersal of his notebooks, made him especially available to later mythmakers.', history:'Leonardo belonged to Renaissance workshop culture, court patronage, military engineering, humanism, and the revival of classical learning. He observed nature with extraordinary patience but worked before the institutional sciences that later readers often project back onto him.', works:'<em>The Last Supper</em>, <em>Mona Lisa</em>, <em>Virgin of the Rocks</em>, and the <em>Vitruvian Man</em> are among the most studied images in art. The Codex Atlanticus, Codex Leicester, and anatomical sheets preserve designs and observations that range from canal systems to the human heart.', ideas:'Leonardo’s ideas were rooted in proportion, motion, light, and the visible operations of nature. He did not found an occult order, but his interest in harmony and correspondences makes him easy to enlist in Hermetic stories. Modern claims about coded messages in <em>The Last Supper</em> are largely products of popular fiction and visual over-reading rather than Renaissance documentation.', influence:'He changed painting through sfumato, composition, and the treatment of the body; he also became a model for the universal investigator. Engineers, anatomists, artists, and designers continue to use his notebooks as symbols of cross-disciplinary attention.', controversies:'Authenticity disputes, lost-work legends, and conspiracy narratives about secret teachings frequently attach to Leonardo. The most famous recent versions turn him into guardian of a suppressed sacred bloodline. They rely on imaginative connections, but they also reveal the irresistible appeal of a figure who left so many densely coded-looking pages.', legacy:'Leonardo is not merely a “mystery genius.” He is a record of sustained looking. His occult afterlife grows from that visual intensity: the sense that every line, mirror-written note, and unfinished face conceals a second world.', sources:'<ul><li>Leonardo da Vinci, notebooks and Codex Leicester</li><li>Royal Collection Trust, Leonardo drawings</li><li>Musée du Louvre, <em>Mona Lisa</em> materials</li><li>Martin Kemp, <em>Leonardo da Vinci: The Marvellous Works of Nature and Man</em></li><li>Walter Isaacson, <em>Leonardo da Vinci</em></li><li>Biblioteca Ambrosiana, Codex Atlanticus resources</li></ul>' },
    'wolfgang-amadeus-mozart': { description:'Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart was a composer of startling range whose membership in Freemasonry, and the symbolic world of <em>The Magic Flute</em>, made him an enduring figure of esoteric musical legend.', biography:'Mozart was born in Salzburg in 1756, the son of Leopold Mozart, and toured Europe as a celebrated child performer with his sister Nannerl. He worked under the Salzburg archbishop, broke with that employment, and built an unusually independent career in Vienna as composer, performer, teacher, and publisher. He died in Vienna in 1791 at thirty-five.\n\nHis final years were financially pressured but artistically prolific. Opera, chamber music, concertos, sacred works, and the unfinished <em>Requiem</em> were all written within a compressed life that later admirers turned into a tragic myth of divine genius.', history:'Mozart lived in the Habsburg Enlightenment, when Joseph II’s reforms, salons, theatres, and Masonic lodges created new social spaces for artists and educated professionals. Freemasonry was regulated and periodically suspect, but it supplied a language of fraternity, moral improvement, and symbolic ritual.', works:'His works include <em>The Marriage of Figaro</em>, <em>Don Giovanni</em>, <em>Così fan tutte</em>, <em>The Magic Flute</em>, the Jupiter Symphony, piano concertos, string quartets, and the <em>Requiem</em>. <em>The Magic Flute</em>, written with Emanuel Schikaneder, uses trials, temples, priests, and light-versus-darkness imagery that make it central to Masonic discussion.', ideas:'Mozart joined the lodge Zur Wohltätigkeit and composed music for Masonic occasions. That real affiliation should not be inflated into a hidden master-plan. His lodge music and <em>The Magic Flute</em> draw on the moral allegory and theatrical symbolism of Enlightenment Freemasonry, where wisdom was imagined as an ethical discipline.', influence:'Mozart became a touchstone for Romantic ideas of genius, for opera, symphonic form, and musical education. Masonic musicians and scholars continue to study his lodge pieces as unusually vivid musical documents of the period.', controversies:'The story that Antonio Salieri murdered Mozart is a durable fiction, popularized by Pushkin and later <em>Amadeus</em>. The mysterious commissioner of the <em>Requiem</em>, Mozart’s death, and Masonic symbolism have produced other tales, but the historical Mozart is already more interesting than the conspiratorial version.', legacy:'Mozart’s music remains both transparent and inexhaustible. The Masonic thread in his life gives occult interpreters something real to explore: not secret domination, but eighteenth-century ritual, fraternity, music, and moral theatre.', sources:'<ul><li>Neue Mozart-Ausgabe, works and documents</li><li>Maynard Solomon, <em>Mozart: A Life</em></li><li>Jan Swafford, <em>Mozart: The Reign of Love</em></li><li>Stanley Sadie, <em>Mozart: The Early Years</em></li><li>W. A. Mozart, <em>The Magic Flute</em></li><li>International Mozarteum Foundation, Mozart biography</li></ul>' },
    'ludwig-van-beethoven': { description:'Ludwig van Beethoven carried the Classical style into a new, stormier conception of musical selfhood. His deafness, private notebooks, and heroic reputation later made him a symbolic figure in mystical readings of art.', biography:'Beethoven was baptized in Bonn in 1770 and moved to Vienna in 1792, where he studied in the orbit of Haydn, Salieri, and the city’s great performers. He first became known as a virtuoso pianist, then as a composer of piano sonatas, chamber music, symphonies, and large public works. Progressive hearing loss became devastatingly apparent by the early 1800s, but it did not end his compositional life.\n\nThe Heiligenstadt Testament of 1802 records his despair and decision to continue for art. In his later years he withdrew from ordinary social life, communicated through conversation books, and wrote the late quartets, the <em>Missa solemnis</em>, and the Ninth Symphony. He died in Vienna in 1827.', history:'Beethoven lived through the French Revolution, Napoleonic wars, Congress of Vienna, and the rise of a public concert culture. His patrons still mattered, but his career helped establish the composer as an autonomous and morally serious artist rather than a court servant.', works:'The <em>Eroica</em>, Fifth, Sixth, Seventh, and Ninth Symphonies; the “Moonlight,” “Waldstein,” and “Hammerklavier” sonatas; <em>Fidelio</em>; the <em>Missa solemnis</em>; and the late string quartets constitute a canon of exceptional range. The Ninth’s setting of Schiller’s “Ode to Joy” brought choral, philosophical, and civic aspiration into symphonic form.', ideas:'Beethoven did not write occult doctrine, yet his music has often been treated as spiritual initiation: darkness becoming light, struggle becoming freedom, silence becoming inner hearing. He kept notebooks of quotations and spiritual reflections, and his late works invite symbolic reading, though their difficulty should not be mistaken for a coded secret society.', influence:'His formal experiments transformed symphonic and chamber music and became a burden as well as an inspiration for nineteenth-century composers. Romantic artists made “Beethoven” into an ideal of the suffering visionary.', controversies:'The circumstances of his health, relationships, “Immortal Beloved” letter, and death have encouraged medical and romantic mysteries. The claim that particular musical motifs encode hidden esoteric messages is less important than the genuine sacred intensity later listeners find in the work.', legacy:'Beethoven’s legacy is the idea that music can record an individual wrestling with fate. That is why his work remains so attractive to mystical readers: it makes moral and emotional transformation audible.', sources:'<ul><li>Beethoven-Haus Bonn, digital archive</li><li>Maynard Solomon, <em>Beethoven</em></li><li>Jan Swafford, <em>Beethoven: Anguish and Triumph</em></li><li>Lewis Lockwood, <em>Beethoven: The Music and the Life</em></li><li>Ludwig van Beethoven, Heiligenstadt Testament</li><li>British Library, Beethoven manuscripts</li></ul>' },
    'vincent-van-gogh': { description:'Vincent van Gogh was a Dutch painter whose religious searching, letters, and luminous late work created a posthumous image of the artist as visionary, martyr, and seer.', biography:'Vincent van Gogh was born in Zundert in the Netherlands in 1853. Before becoming an artist he worked in the art trade, taught, and attempted religious ministry among miners in the Borinage. In 1880 he committed himself to art, relying heavily on the material and emotional support of his brother Theo. He worked in the Netherlands, Paris, Arles, Saint-Rémy, and Auvers-sur-Oise, producing an astonishing body of paintings and drawings in little more than a decade.\n\nVan Gogh struggled with recurrent illness and periods of acute crisis. After the famous ear injury in Arles, he voluntarily entered the asylum at Saint-Rémy, where he painted cypresses, olive trees, and <em>The Starry Night</em>. He died in 1890 after a gunshot wound, at thirty-seven.', history:'His development followed the rise of modern art, Japanese prints, Impressionism, Divisionism, and the new art markets of Paris. He was not isolated from his time: he corresponded with Theo, worked alongside Gauguin, studied prints and color theory, and thought deeply about religion and labor.', works:'Major works include <em>The Potato Eaters</em>, the Sunflowers, <em>The Bedroom</em>, <em>Irises</em>, <em>The Starry Night</em>, and the portraits of Dr Gachet. His letters to Theo and others are a major artistic document, revealing deliberate choices about color, drawing, faith, books, and the desired social role of art.', ideas:'Van Gogh’s earliest impulse was evangelical, and the spiritual charge never left his work even after he abandoned formal ministry. Stars, wheat fields, trees, and night skies became signs of an animated cosmos. Later audiences have called him a mystic or visionary; those words describe the intensity of his attention, not membership in a concealed occult system.', influence:'His color, brushwork, and emotional directness helped shape Expressionism, Fauvism, and modern ideas of the artist. His letters have been as influential as his paintings, creating a rare record of artistic thought in motion.', controversies:'The ear episode, diagnosis, and death remain subjects of debate, often oversimplified into the stereotype of the mad genius. Occult readings sometimes turn the painted stars into astronomical prophecy or coded mysticism. The letters provide a better guide: an artist who searched for consolation in nature, books, work, and human connection.', legacy:'Van Gogh’s work makes ordinary fields and rooms feel charged with spiritual weather. His posthumous fame became immense because viewers recognize both craft and vulnerability in the paintings, and because the life seems to ask whether vision can survive suffering.', sources:'<ul><li>Van Gogh Museum, biography and collection records</li><li>Vincent van Gogh, <em>Vincent van Gogh: The Letters</em></li><li>Steven Naifeh and Gregory White Smith, <em>Van Gogh: The Life</em></li><li>Ronald de Leeuw, <em>The Letters of Vincent van Gogh</em></li><li>Kröller-Müller Museum, Van Gogh collection</li><li>Musée d’Orsay, Van Gogh collection essays</li></ul>' }
  };
  for (const [slug, patch] of Object.entries(profiles)) {
    const figure = occultRumorFigures.find(item => item.slug === slug);
    if (figure) Object.assign(figure, patch);
  }
  route();
})();
